{"authored" [{"question" "e-3744" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x) = x^3-6x^2-15x.$ Given that $f'(5) = 0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f''(5)  >           0$\n\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=5$\n\n- $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=5$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033145/example-3744.json" "file_sha256" "ee17bd7030b3b511f10fe17bb2baaf4f385d32e8236cf6b0a1746ad20b833d84" "basis" 1229 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033145/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-3744" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that $x=5$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)=  x^3-6x^2-15x,$ we get\n\n$$\nf'(x) =  3x^2-12x-15\n$$\n\nWe're given that $f'(5) = 0.$ Indeed, we can see that $f'(5) = 3(5)^2 - 12(5) - 15 = 0.$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =6x-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(5) & =6(5)-12 \\\\  & =30-12 \\\\  & =18>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is positive, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033145/example-3744.json" "file_sha256" "ee17bd7030b3b511f10fe17bb2baaf4f385d32e8236cf6b0a1746ad20b833d84" "basis" 1229 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033145/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-3743" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the $x$-values of the stationary points of $f(x) =  x^4 - 8 x ^ 2 + 2$ and classify each point." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033145/example-3743.json" "file_sha256" "2408a90c9c5759e888fdec1621891586d532b8cda3c26befcf3e65418487e564" "basis" 1229 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033145/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-3743" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x) = x^4 - 8 x ^ 2 + 2,$ we get\n\n$$\nf'(x)  = 4x^3 - 16 x,\n$$\n\nand solving $f'(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3}-16x & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}-4x & =0 \\\\ x({x}^{2}-4) & =0 \\\\ x(x+2)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have stationary points at $x=0$ and $x=\\pm 2.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of $f''(x)$ at the critical points. Computing $f''(x),$ we get\n\n$$\nf''(x)= 12 x^2 - 16,\n$$\n\nand evaluating $f''(x)$ at each critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-2) & =12(-2)^{2}-16=32>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(0) & =12(0)^{2}-16=-16<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(2) & =12(2)^{2}-16=32>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe second derivative is negative at $x=0,$ so this is a relative maximum. On the other hand, the second derivative is positive at $x=\\pm 2,$ so these are relative minima." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033145/example-3743.json" "file_sha256" "2408a90c9c5759e888fdec1621891586d532b8cda3c26befcf3e65418487e564" "basis" 1229 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033145/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-21504" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the function $f(x)=-6{x}^{2}+36x-48,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=3$ is a critical point of $f(x)$\n- ${f}^{″}(3)<0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=3$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040760/state.json" "file_sha256" "caa325fdb04ffbcc3111224bcbefc1ff4dd3a40fd6ca40c894061ce544e44fdd" "basis" 1459 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040760/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-21504" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to identify whether $x=3$ is a critical point. If it is, we then need to classify it using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=-6{x}^{2}+36x-48,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-12x+36,\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-12x+36 & =0 \\\\ -12(x-3) & =0 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have a critical point at $x=3.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at this critical point. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=-12,\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=3$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(3)=-12<0.\n$$\n\nThe second derivative is negative at $x=3,$ so this critical point is a relative *maximum*. \n\nTherefore, comparing our results with the given statements, we conclude that:\n\n- $x=3$ is a critical point of $f(x)\\,✓$\n\n- ${f}^{″}(3)<0\\,✓$\n\n- $f(x)$ has a relative *maximum* at $x=3\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040760/state.json" "file_sha256" "caa325fdb04ffbcc3111224bcbefc1ff4dd3a40fd6ca40c894061ce544e44fdd" "basis" 1459 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040760/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-21504" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040760/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6c9349efcf2e2ba73a2cac1d6ef9ca0823df40ccb84e5bf774a2146ecf20be58" "basis" 1459 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040760/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-21504" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040760/state.json" "file_sha256" "caa325fdb04ffbcc3111224bcbefc1ff4dd3a40fd6ca40c894061ce544e44fdd" "basis" 1459 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040760/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-21504" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040760/state.json" "file_sha256" "caa325fdb04ffbcc3111224bcbefc1ff4dd3a40fd6ca40c894061ce544e44fdd" "basis" 1459 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040760/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-21504" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040760/state.json" "file_sha256" "caa325fdb04ffbcc3111224bcbefc1ff4dd3a40fd6ca40c894061ce544e44fdd" "basis" 1459 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040760/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-21504" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040760/state.json" "file_sha256" "caa325fdb04ffbcc3111224bcbefc1ff4dd3a40fd6ca40c894061ce544e44fdd" "basis" 1459 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040760/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-21504" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040760/state.json" "file_sha256" "caa325fdb04ffbcc3111224bcbefc1ff4dd3a40fd6ca40c894061ce544e44fdd" "basis" 1459 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040760/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-21504" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "None of the statements are true"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040760/state.json" "file_sha256" "caa325fdb04ffbcc3111224bcbefc1ff4dd3a40fd6ca40c894061ce544e44fdd" "basis" 1459 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040760/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-21504" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040760/state.json" "file_sha256" "caa325fdb04ffbcc3111224bcbefc1ff4dd3a40fd6ca40c894061ce544e44fdd" "basis" 1459 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040760/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-21814" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=4{x}^{5}+5{x}^{4}-10{x}^{2}-20x+30.$ Given that ${f}^{′}(-1)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{″}(-1)<0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=-1$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=-1$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040760/state.json" "file_sha256" "caa325fdb04ffbcc3111224bcbefc1ff4dd3a40fd6ca40c894061ce544e44fdd" "basis" 1459 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040760/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-21814" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that $x=-1$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)=4{x}^{5}+5{x}^{4}-10{x}^{2}-20x+30,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=20({x}^{4}+{x}^{3}-x-1).\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=20(4{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-1).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-1) & =20(4(-1)^{3}+3(-1)^{2}-1) \\\\  & =20(-4+3-1) \\\\  & =-40<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040760/state.json" "file_sha256" "caa325fdb04ffbcc3111224bcbefc1ff4dd3a40fd6ca40c894061ce544e44fdd" "basis" 1459 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040760/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-21814" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040760/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6c9349efcf2e2ba73a2cac1d6ef9ca0823df40ccb84e5bf774a2146ecf20be58" "basis" 1459 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040760/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-21814" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040760/state.json" "file_sha256